<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/meeting-table.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/meeting-table-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/meeting-table.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/">Guides</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">The Michigan Appraisal Clause Explained in Plain English</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Guide · 7 min read · August 4, 2026</p><h1>The Michigan Appraisal Clause Explained in Plain English</h1><p class="ph-lede">What the appraisal provision in every Michigan property policy says, how it works step by step, and what it can’t do.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why every Michigan property policy has one</h2>
<p>If you own a home, condo or commercial building in Michigan, your property policy almost certainly contains an appraisal clause. That isn’t a coincidence. Michigan’s Insurance Code, at MCL 500.2833, requires fire and property policies issued in the state to include an appraisal provision. Insurers word it slightly differently, but the core idea is the same everywhere from Monroe to Copper Harbor: when you and the insurer can’t agree on what the damage is worth, there’s a way to settle the number without filing a lawsuit.</p>
<p>This article explains the Michigan appraisal clause in general terms. It isn’t legal advice, and your own policy wording controls the details, so read it alongside this guide.</p>
<h2>What the clause says, in general</h2>
<p>The statutory framework works roughly like this. If the insured and insurer fail to agree on the actual cash value or the amount of loss, either side may make a written demand for appraisal. Each side then selects a competent, independent appraiser, and the statute sets a 20-day window after the demand to do so. The two appraisers then have 15 days to select an umpire. If they can’t agree on one, a court of record in Michigan can be asked to pick the umpire. Finally, a written award signed by any two of the three determines the amount of loss.</p>
<p>That “any two of three” rule is what makes appraisal efficient. The appraisers often reach agreement on most items themselves, and the umpire only weighs in on what’s left. If one appraiser and the umpire sign, that’s enough.</p>
<h3>Key terms worth knowing</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Actual cash value</strong>: replacement cost minus depreciation, the figure many policies pay first.</li>
<li><strong>Amount of loss</strong>: the dollar value of the covered damage, which the award sets.</li>
<li><strong>Competent and independent</strong>: the appraiser has the skill for the job and no stake in the result.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">claim terms glossary</a> covers dozens more.</p>
<h2>What appraisal can and can’t decide</h2>
<p>Appraisal decides the amount of loss. It does not decide coverage. If the insurer says a roof leak is excluded wear and tear, or that a basement flood is surface water rather than sewer backup, that is a coverage dispute, and an appraisal panel generally isn’t the place to resolve it. Appraisal also doesn’t award penalty interest, attorney fees or damages for how the claim was handled.</p>
<p>Where it shines is in disputes about quantity and price: how many squares of shingles a Lansing colonial needs, what drywall and paint cost in Traverse City this year, whether a damaged hardwood floor can be refinished or must be replaced, or what a smoke-damaged sofa was worth. Those questions turn on measurements, pricing and construction knowledge, which is what appraisers and umpires bring.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Coverage versus amount</b>If the insurer has denied the claim entirely, appraisal usually isn’t the next step. Talk with a Michigan attorney about coverage questions first.</div>
<h2>How a typical appraisal unfolds</h2>
<ol>
<li>You compare estimates and confirm the gap is about money, not coverage.</li>
<li>You send a written demand that cites the appraisal provision and your claim number.</li>
<li>Each side names its appraiser, generally within 20 days.</li>
<li>The appraisers agree on an umpire, generally within 15 days, or ask a court to appoint one.</li>
<li>The appraisers inspect, exchange estimates and settle what they can.</li>
<li>The umpire decides the remaining items, and two of three sign the award.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/michigan-appraisal-process/">step-by-step appraisal process guide</a> walks through each stage in more depth.</p>
<h2>Costs under the clause</h2>
<p>Most policies say each side pays its own appraiser and the two sides split the umpire’s fee. Read your clause to confirm. In an illustrative example, if the insurer’s estimate is $18,000 and your contractor’s is $41,000, a $23,000 gap may justify the cost of appraisal; a $2,500 gap might not. Our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> helps you run those numbers for your own claim.</p>
<h2>Deadlines that sit next to the clause</h2>
<p>The same section of the Insurance Code generally sets a suit-limitation rule: an action generally must be started within one year after the loss, or a longer period if the policy allows, with that period tolled from when the insured gives notice until the insurer formally denies liability. Appraisal and the suit deadline are separate, so don’t assume one pauses the other. Ask a Michigan attorney how the timing applies to you, and use our <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/claim-deadline-planner/">claim deadline planner</a> to keep your dates in one place.</p>
<h2>After the award</h2>
<p>Once signed, the award is usually paid under the policy’s loss payment terms, minus the deductible and prior payments. Replacement cost policies often hold back recoverable depreciation until repairs are done. If payment drags on, MCL 500.2006 provides for 12% annual penalty interest on certain claims not paid on time, though whether it applies depends on the facts. Michigan courts generally haven’t recognized a separate first-party bad-faith tort, so questions about claim handling belong with a Michigan attorney.</p>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>The Michigan appraisal clause is a practical tool for one specific problem: a covered loss where the two sides can’t agree on the number. Read your policy, document the gap carefully and choose an appraiser who is truly independent. If you’d like a second set of eyes, our free initial review can tell you whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/appraiser-public-adjuster-or-attorney/"><small>Guide</small><span>Appraiser, Public Adjuster or Attorney: Who Fits Your Claim?</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/ice-dam-claims-in-michigan/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Dam Claims in Michigan: Roofs, Ceilings and Disputes</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/basement-backup-coverage-gaps/"><small>Guide</small><span>Basement Backup Coverage Gaps Michigan Homeowners Miss</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article>
